---
title: Prawn Fried Rice
servings: 1
course: dinner
cuisine: Thai
tags: quick, stir-fry, seafood, rice
image: https://img.youtube.com/vi/M-PuGg7Ek-w/maxresdefault.jpg
source: https://youtu.be/M-PuGg7Ek-w
time: 15m
---

Make the dipping sauce by combining @fish sauce{2%tbsp}, finely sliced @bird's eye chilli{1}, juice of @lime{1}, and @sugar{1%tsp} in a small #bowl{}. Stir to dissolve the sugar and set aside.

Slice @shallot{1} lengthwise. Smash and finely chop @garlic{1%clove}. Separate the stems and leaves of @choy sum{100%g}; roughly slice the greens and cut the stems into 1 cm batons. Slice @cucumber{0.5} into rounds for garnish.

Heat a #wok{} over high heat until smoking hot. Add @peanut oil{1.5%tbsp}, then add the sliced shallot and choy sum stems. Stir-fry constantly to get some heat into them, then add @salt{1%pinch}.

Add the chopped garlic and peeled, deveined @raw prawn{150%g}. Stir-fry using a #spatula{} for ~{45%seconds} until the prawns begin turning pink and opaque.

Push everything to one side of the wok to create clear space. Add @&peanut oil{0.5%tbsp} into the gap and let it get hot. Crack in @egg{1}, let it sit for a few seconds, break the yolk, and stir it through with the rest of the ingredients.

Add cold day-old @cooked rice{250%g}, @oyster sauce{1%tbsp}, @white pepper{1%pinch}, and the reserved choy sum greens. Stir-fry and toss vigorously until the rice is heated through and everything is evenly coated.

Transfer to a #plate{}. Garnish with the sliced cucumber and serve alongside the dipping sauce.
